    "data": {
      "A": "Build on the fastest measured design, candidate 9661742815304083 (0.0715 s): store the shorter dimension contiguously, incrementally update band sums, and use the generation-stamped open-addressed frequency table. Research low-overhead refinements to this serial kernel and its table reuse; preserve full-input correctness, signed prefix sums, and a 64-bit answer. Benchmark end to end against this candidate, not just isolated operations.",
      "B": "Investigate a distinct serial alternative for the per-band prefix-frequency scan, such as coordinate-compressed contiguous counts, and compare it with the measured open-addressed table. Any dimension-based dispatch must retain a sound general fallback and avoid worsening larger cases. The measured map-reserve/ivdep and 2D-prefix variants were slower (0.2296 s and 0.2482 s), so do not assume those changes help; preserve incremental band sums and validate all cases.",
      "C": "Research parallelizing the faster short-dimension, contiguous-band kernel rather than only the original map-based implementation. Band pairs are independent; compare suitable static or dynamic work distribution and a serial cutoff, using private frequency state and a correct 64-bit reduction. The measured OpenMP candidate took 0.0962 s—better than baseline but slower than the 0.0715 s serial candidate—so retain parallelism only if integrated, end-to-end measurements show a win on the four-core ARM64 target.",
      "G": "Integrate the best correct programs from A, B, and C, using candidate 9661742815304083 (0.0715 s) as the current performance reference. Measure each promising integration as a whole: gains may not combine, and added parallelism or dispatch may regress performance. Keep the fastest validated implementation, preserve correctness for every valid input including negative elements, and do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or source outside the permitted integration.",
      "analysis": "The official task counts submatrices summing to k, with dimensions below 1000 and potentially negative entries. The baseline is correct at 0.2500 s. The strongest measured candidate is 9661742815304083 at 0.0715 s (about 3.5× the baseline speedup), using shorter-dimension pairing, contiguous scans, and a generation-stamped hash table. The measured OpenMP version is 0.0962 s; other listed variants are about 0.2296–0.2482 s. Thus research should start from the serial winner, test alternatives empirically, and make no assumption that optimizations compose."
    },
